```python
import math

def dies_per_wafer(area, d=300.0):
    return int(math.pi * (d / 2) ** 2 / area - math.pi * d / math.sqrt(2 * area))

def poisson_yield(area, d0):
    return math.exp(-area / 100 * d0)

def cost_per_good_die(wafer_cost, area, d0):
    return wafer_cost / (dies_per_wafer(area) * poisson_yield(area, d0))

MONOLITHIC = cost_per_good_die(25000, 800, 0.2)
CHIPLETS = 4 * cost_per_good_die(25000, 200, 0.2)
```

A 300 mm wafer holds 64 dies of 800 mm², of which $e^{-1.6} \approx 20\%$ are good at $D_0 = 0.2$: about USD 1,935 per good die. It holds 306 dies of 200 mm², $e^{-0.4} \approx 67\%$ good, so four good chiplets cost about USD 488 — a quarter of the monolithic cost. Real chiplet designs pay some of this back in packaging, die-to-die interfaces and the extra area those interfaces need, which is the trade-off of the next lesson.

